===Noun===
{{en-noun|+|cameræ<l:rare>|camerae<l:rare>}}

# {{senseid|en|Q15328}}{{lb|en|photography}} A device for taking still or moving pictures or [[photograph|photographs]].
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/fenella-saunders Fenella Saunders]|title=Tiny Lenses See the Big Picture|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/tiny-lenses-see-the-big-picture|magazine=w:American Scientist|passage=The single-imaging optic of the mammalian eye offers some distinct visual advantages. Such lenses can take in photons from a wide range of angles, increasing light sensitivity. They also have high spatial resolution, resolving incoming images in minute detail. It’s therefore not surprising that most '''cameras''' mimic this arrangement.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130907101516/https://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/tiny-lenses-see-the-big-picture|archivedate=7 September 2013}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Jessie Yeung|title=Hong Kong plans to install thousands of surveillance cameras. Critics say it’s more proof the city is moving closer to China|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2024/10/05/asia/hong-kong-police-cameras-facial-recognition-intl-hnk-dst/index.html|date=2024-10-05|passage=Glance up while strolling through parts of downtown Hong Kong and, chances are, you’ll notice the glassy black lens of a surveillance '''camera''' trained on the city’s crowded streets.<br>And that sight will become more common in the coming years, as the city’s police pursue an ambitious campaign to install thousands of '''cameras''' to elevate their surveillance capabilities.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260302003709/https://edition.cnn.com/2024/10/05/asia/hong-kong-police-cameras-facial-recognition-intl-hnk-dst/index.html|archivedate=2 March 2026}}
# {{lb|en|computer graphics|video games}} The [[viewpoint]] in a [[three-dimensional]] game or [[simulation]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Tom Meigs|title=Ultimate game design: building game worlds|passage=If you're building a third-person game with enclosed or tight spaces, try to figure out up front what '''camera''' problems you will likely encounter. Use this identification process to influence the early building process.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Patrick O'Luanaigh|title=Game Design Complete
|passage=I'm talking about the way the '''camera''' flies up above the skater when you leap into the air. No one had done it before.}}
